  "10.1148/ryai.250651": "Informatics, Named Entity Recognition, Patient Scheduling/No-Show Prediction, Resource Allocation, Impact of AI on Education, Artificial Intelligence, Large Language Models, Agentic AI, Multi-Agent Systems, Radiology Workflow, Clinical Decision Support, Health Care Automation © RSNA, 2026.",
  "10.1016/j.array.2026.100721": "The integration of agentic artificial intelligence (agentic AI) and digital twins (DTs) enables decision-making systems that are intelligent, adaptive, and goal-oriented. This paper advances that convergence through a multilayer integration framework that organizes perception, knowledge and data management, LLM-based reasoning, learning, decision-making, action execution, and feedback adaptation into a cohesive structure. Within this framework, LLM-driven agents act as cognitive cores for context-aware planning and collaboration, while the digital twin provides a shared situational model that maintains real-time state, simulation capabilities, and policy constraints. The contributions are threefold: (i) a novel architecture that operationalizes continuous learning and closed-loop autonomy through tightly coupled agents and a DT; (ii) a use-case demonstration in power balancing for electrical grid management, where agents coordinate demand forecasting, distributed energy resources, and network constraints via a grid digital twin; and (iii) an analysis of key enablers and challenges, such as model synchronization, interpretability, safety, and cognitive load distribution, necessary for scalable and trustworthy deployment. The proposed integration offers a principled path to resilient, transparent, and data-efficient decision-making systems for cyber–physical infrastructure and provides a foundation for extending agentic AI-DT synergies to other safety-critical domains. • Integrates agentic AI with digital twins for adaptive decision-making. • Uses LLMs as cognitive cores for reasoning and goal-oriented autonomy. • Enables continuous learning through real-time feedback and simulation.",
  "10.3390/agriengineering8010008": "Artificial intelligence (AI) shows great promise for transforming the agriculture sector and can enable the development of many modern farming practices over conventional methods. Nowadays, AI agents and agentic AI have attained popularity due to their autonomous structure and working mechanism. This research work proposes an agentic AI framework that integrates multiple agents developed for farming land to promote climate-smart agriculture and support United Nations (UN) sustainable development goals (SDGs). The developed structure has four agents: Agent A for monitoring soil properties, Agent B for weather sensing, Agent C for disease detection vision sensing in rice crops, and Agent D, a multi-agent supervisor agent chatbot connected with the other agents. The overall objective was to connect all agents on a single platform to obtain sensor data and perform a predictive analysis. This will help farmers and landowners obtain information about weather conditions, soil properties, and vision-based disease detection so that appropriate measures can be taken on agricultural land for rice crops. For soil properties (nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium) from Agent A and climate data (temperature and humidity) from Agent B, we deployed the long short-term memory (LSTM), gated recurrent unit (GRU), and one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D-CNN) predictive models, which achieved an accuracy of 93.4%, 94%, and 96% for Agent A; a 0.27 mean absolute error (MAE) for temperature; and a 2.9 MAE for humidity on the Agent B data. For Agent C, we used vision transformer (ViT), MobileViT, and RiceNet (with a diffusion model layer as a feature extractor) models to detect disease. The models achieved accuracies of 95%, 98.5%, and 85.4% during training respectively. Overall, the proposed framework demonstrates how agentic AI can be used to transform conventional farming practices into a digital process, thereby supporting smart agriculture.",
  "10.1007/s10462-025-11458-6": "This research proposes a holistic agentic Artificial Intelligence framework that seeks to solve the palpable requirement of autonomy, versatility, and world-scale generative Artificial Intelligence systems. When framing the concept of an agentic Artificial Intelligence as a paradigm shift where Large Language Models are relatively reactive, tool-augmented to a proactive, modular agent that pursues goals in the long term, the research discusses how large language models are becoming an architectural mandate. The proposed research uses a skillful literature synthesis and develops a concept and technical framework that integrates perception, memory, planning, execution, and communication modules. In contrast to current frameworks like AutoGPT and ReAct, where the deep memory, explainability, and certain control of scalability are often lacking, the proposed framework offers a solution with persistent memory layers, semantic routing, and modular orchestration pipelines when it comes to cloud-native deployments. Experimental verification offers a greater degree of autonomy, coordination, and resilience in a diverse range of activities such as enterprise automation and robotics. It is also an edge deployment with the help of lightweight microservices framework. In practice, this method supports scaled, comprehensible agents adapted to long-loop thinking and human-in-the-loop management and adaptive value chain serving. The novelty of the work is attributed to its reusable architecture, as it is not only capable of modifying agentic behavior alone, but it can also connect the theoretical principles and industry feasibility. Future efforts will be with the integration of ethical governance, uniform benchmarking, and multimodal memory remodeling in next generation of real-world autonomous systems.",
  "10.1038/s41746-026-02453-4": "Download PDFAbstractAgentic artificial intelligence (AI) represents a pivotal shift in clinical decision support, moving beyond static tools by reasoning, adapting, and acting alongside clinicians. Psychiatry, grounded in subjective experience, trust, and longitudinal care, offers both an opportunity and a high-stakes testbed. Agentic systems may enhance documentation, personalize care, support continuous monitoring, and extend access, while raising risks around bias, explainability, privacy, and therapeutic alliance. In this Perspective, we (i) define psychiatry-specific agentic AI distinct from decision-support and fully autonomous systems; (ii) synthesize current evidence across studies; (iii) propose assistive, collaborative, and semi-autonomous roles; and (iv) outline a roadmap for responsible implementation.Similar content being viewed by othersPractical AI application in psychiatry: historical review and future directionsArticleOpen access03 June 2025The role of agentic artificial intelligence in healthcare: a scoping reviewArticleOpen access14 March 2026Responsible and innovative AI for mental health care: five priority themesArticleOpen access20 August 2026Explore related subjectsDiscover the latest articles and news in related subjects.Business and industryComplex networksHealth carePsychologyScientific communitySocial sciencesCognitive Phenomena in Philosophical ContextsIntroductionArtificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming modern healthcare, driving advances in diagnostics, clinical decision support, and personalized treatment strategies1,2. Traditionally, AI systems in medicine have focused on narrow, well-defined tasks, such as image analysis or structured data interpretation operating as static tools that assist clinicians in specific, bounded contexts. However, a new and more dynamic frontier is emerging: Agentic AI3. While AI in psychiatry has been widely discussed, this paper is the first to advance a psychiatry-specific conceptualization of Agentic AI, anchored in the field’s distinctive reliance on narratives, multimodal signals, and therapeutic alliance.Unlike conventional AI models, agentic AI systems possess a degree of autonomy, enabling them to make context-dependent decisions, adapt to real-time data, and proactively engage in task execution with minimal human oversight4. These systems are designed not merely to assist but to act as semi-autonomous “agents” capable of shaping workflows and outcomes. As shown in Fig.1, agentic AI occupies a critical middle ground between decision-support and autonomous systems, offering contextual adaptability without full autonomy. Early successes in domains such as finance5, cybersecurity6, and general medicine7,8illustrate the transformative potential of these systems to augment, rather than replace human expertise, providing more responsive, adaptive, and personalized support.Fig. 1: Agentic AI in psychiatry: positioning and interaction design.Full size imageaThe evolving spectrum of clinical AI, spanning from low-autonomy decision-support systems to high-autonomy autonomous agents. Agentic AI occupies the middle ground, enabling adaptive, multimodal, and context-sensitive support under human oversight. In psychiatry, these systems can augment clinical decision-making without replacing therapeutic relationships.bA human-centered interaction loop illustrating continuous data ingestion, AI-driven synthesis, clinician review, patient-facing actions, and iterative feedback. The loop underscores co-development, trust-building, and safety in agent deployment.bCreated in BioRender. Bhat, V. (2025)https://BioRender.com/ybt9e6p. Abbreviations: PHQ-9 Patient Health Questionnaire-9, BDI Beck Depression Inventory, AI-MH Artificial Intelligence for Mental Health, Rx-GPT Prescription—Generative Pre-trained Transformer.Mental health services worldwide face increasing demand, driven by rising prevalence of mental disorders and an urgent shortage of trained professionals9. Long wait times, limited access in underserved regions, and clinician burnout have created significant gaps in care delivery10. In this context, agentic AI offers the promise of supporting clinicians in triaging patients, continuously monitoring mental health trajectories, and even delivering certain forms of low-intensity digital interventions. Psychiatry is uniquely nuanced, relying heavily on subjective narratives, contextual interpretation, and trust-based therapeutic relationships. Integrating agentic AI must therefore prioritize clinical accuracy, ethical accountability, and patient safety, while also safeguarding data privacy and ensuring alignment with professional psychiatric standards and core human values11. Importantly, psychiatric care is characterized by variable patient adherence and frequent need for real-time clinician guidance. For this reason, the agentic AI systems discussed in this article are envisioned as constrained, human-in-the-loop systems, with bounded autonomy, where independence is deliberately limited to low- and moderate-risk tasks limited to low- and moderate-risk tasks (e.g., symptom monitoring, micro-interventions, triage suggestions). In all high-risk contexts and in patients with severe psychiatric disorders, the system escalates decisions back to clinicians.This article provides: (i) a psychiatry-specific definition of agentic AI, (ii) a systematic and critical synthesis of current applications, (iii) a framework of clinical roles and limitations, and (iv) a roadmap for responsible implementation. By balancing promise with pitfalls, we aim to catalyze rigorous, grounded dialog across psychiatry, ethics, and AI design, and provide a foundation for future research and policy development in this rapidly evolving area.Defining agentic AI: the need for standardizationDespite growing interest in AI applications across medicine, the term “Agentic AI” remains conceptually ambiguous and inconsistently used across different fields. In computer science and human-computer interaction literature, agents are typically described as systems that exhibit autonomy, reactivity, proactivity, and adaptability, characteristics that allow them to perceive their environment, make decisions, and act upon those decisions over time4,12.In some contexts, “agentic AI” refers to systems that possess a limited degree of autonomy in specific decision-making tasks, operating under constrained scenarios with predictable outputs5. In other cases, it is used to describe AI-driven assistants that primarily function as enhanced support tools, augmenting human workflows without taking independent initiative6. This conceptual fluidity creates confusion not only in research and clinical translation but also in policy-making and regulatory discussions.It is therefore, helpful to distinguish Agentic AI from two adjacent concepts:Decision-support AI refers to systems that provide recommendations or augment human judgment but rely on static or rule-based models.Autonomous AI, in contrast, denotes systems capable of making independent decisions and executing actions without human oversight.Agentic AI occupies a middle ground, characterized by its dynamic and adaptive capabilities but operating under explicit human supervision and oversight.To bring greater conceptual clarity, we propose a psychiatry-specific taxonomy of agentic AI roles and their cognitive effects on clinicians and patients13,14. (1) Assistive agents: tools that capture and synthesize narrative data (e.g., therapy transcripts, progress notes) and support clinicians through documentation and longitudinal monitoring, with primary risks related to cognitive offloading and reliance through workflow optimization. (2) Collaborative agents: systems that function as active partners in the care team, integrating multimodal signals and contributing to differential diagnosis or treatment planning while explicitly supporting, rather than substituting, therapeutic alliance, but may introduce authority gradients and anchoring effects that shape clinician judgment over time and, (3) Semi-autonomous agents: systems that can interact directly with patients for intake, triage, or low-intensity interventions, but remain subject to clinician validation to protect safety and context, necessitating tighter constraints, escalation thresholds, and auditability. “Assistive,” “collaborative,” and “semi-autonomous” are best understood as progressive levels of autonomy rather than mutually exclusive system types. These categories describe modes of deployment that a single agentic system may occupy depending on the task. For example, a system may operate in an assistive mode for documentation, shift to a collaborative mode when supporting case formulation with clinicians, and function in a semi-autonomous mode for low-risk tasks such as scheduling reminders or collecting symptom data each with distinct cognitive and safety implications.Based on these considerations, we propose the following definition tailored specifically to psychiatric care:“Agentic AI in psychiatry is an AI system capable of independently gathering, synthesizing, and interpreting multimodal psychiatric data, including narratives, speech, behavioral patterns, and physiological markers, to proactively support clinical decision-making while operating under human oversight and explicitly designed constraints that limit automation bias, over-reliance, and inappropriate authority transfer, and without autonomous diagnostic or treatment authority.”Unlike generic agent definitions, this formulation explicitly anchors agentic AI to psychiatry’s distinctive requirements. First, these systems must be able to work with narrative-rich and unstructured inputs (e.g., patient stories, therapy transcripts), not just structured medical data. Second, they must be designed to preserve and even strengthen relational trust, given psychiatry’s reliance on therapeutic alliance as a predictor of outcomes. Third, evaluation must extend beyond accuracy metrics to include measures such as alliance proxies, patient engagement, and equity impacts. We distinguish agentic AI from autonomous AI not only through the presence of human oversight but also through their underlying modes of operation and the constraints within which they function. Agentic systems are designed to form and pursue task-level goals, plan and execute multi-step sequences of actions, maintain state and memory over time, and operate under explicit human-specified constraints such as configurable levels of autonomy, safety guardrails, and routinized escalation to clinicians. Importantly, human oversight does not by itself prevent automation bias15,16; its effectiveness depends on interaction design choices such as review timing, information granularity, framing, and override mechanisms17,18.Background and related workA comprehensive and structured literature search was conducted in MEDLINE, Embase, and PsycINFO via Ovid from inception to 28 July 2025 to identify original research on agentic and conversational AI in mental health care. This review followed PRISMA guidelines and was registered in PROSPERO (Reg No: CRD420251134540). The following search strategy was used: (agentic artificial intelligence or conversational AI or conversational agents or artificial intelligence copilot or multi-agentic AI or autonomous artificial intelligence or autonomous AI) AND (psychiatry or mental health or behavioral health or mental disorder or mental illness). The full search strings, inclusion/exclusion criteria, and bias assessment approach are provided in the Supplementary Document. A total of 28 studies were included, as summarized in the PRISMA flow diagram (Fig.2). Detailed study characteristics and limitations are presented in Supplementary Tables1,2. Emerging work on conversational and agentic AI in mental health spans retrospective modeling, clinical trials, observational deployments, and case series. Retrospective studies show diagnostic and directive utility, including depression severity estimation with speech-enhanced LLMs19and agent behaviors shaping risk referral20. Trials of chatbots such as Woebot21and W-MA-0222report short-term improvements on validated measures (e.g., PHQ-9, GAD-7) across depression, anxiety, panic, ADHD, and eating disorders, with strong engagement; studies in oncology survivorship, substance use, and chronic pain show mixed but generally favorable outcomes23,24,25. Prompt and policy adaptations, including motivational interviewing scaffolds applied to GPT-4, suggest interaction design can influence adherence, alliance proxies, and behavior change. Observational evidence links real-world use to improved recovery and continuity and to operational gains, with systems like Limbic Access26and Wysa27enabling therapist-level outcome visibility; youth-focused case series (e.g., MYLO28) demonstrate usability and measurable clinical benefits. Collectively, conversational systems deliver credible short-term symptom and workflow gains. Yet the evidence is limited by small sample sizes, high dropout, and under-reporting of negative findings. The next step is to move beyond narrow chatbots toward agentic AI that plans tasks, integrates multimodal signals, and collaborates longitudinally under human oversight. This requires not only technical architectures but also rigorous evaluation against benchmarks of access, equity, and safety, not just accuracy.Fig. 2: PRISMA flow diagram of study selection.Full size imageFlowchart depicting the identification, screening, eligibility assessment, and inclusion of studies according to PRISMA guidelines. The diagram outlines the number of records retrieved, duplicates removed, full texts assessed, and final studies included in the review.Potential benefits and future horizons of agentic AI in psychiatryAgentic AI introduces capabilities that extend beyond those of conventional digital or rules-based systems, offering the potential to support more adaptive, continuous, and context-aware psychiatric care. One of the most immediate opportunities for agentic AI in psychiatry lies in automating and enhancing clinical documentation29. Unlike traditional AI transcription tools that passively record and summarize encounters, agentic AI systems can actively extract, synthesize, and highlight clinically relevant information, identifying inconsistencies or subtle symptom changes over time30. This dynamic approach reduces administrative burden and allows clinicians to dedicate more time to direct patient care and therapeutic alliance. At the same time, prior experience with automated documentation tools shows that clinicians may distrust systems that offer insufficient transparency or generate errors, underscoring the need for explainability and user control.In addition to documentation, agentic AI can support dynamic, real-time psychiatric assessments. By integrating multimodal data, including electronic health records, clinical notes, audio signals (tone, speech patterns, semantics), and even visual cues such as facial expressions and eye movements, agentic systems can construct richer, longitudinal models of patient affect, behavior, and symptom trajectories31. This continuous synthesis enables more nuanced and individualized clinical insights, supporting early detection of symptom exacerbations or treatment side effects. However, integrating such multimodal pipelines at scale is technically demanding, and false positives in monitoring may increase patient anxiety or clinician workload if not carefully managed.Agentic AI also holds significant promise in advancing precision psychiatry. Rather than relying solely on static, guideline-based treatment recommendations, agentic systems can monitor patient progress continuously and adapt interventions accordingly. For example, subtle shifts in speech patterns or physiological markers could inform tailored adjustments in pharmacological or psychotherapeutic approaches31. Recent studies have shown that AI-driven therapists can perform comparably to human clinicians in delivering certain mental health interventions32,33,34, highlighting the potential for agentic AI to extend and reinforce existing care frameworks rather than supplant them. Yet these studies typically involve short-term, low-intensity interventions; evidence for sustained benefits in complex or severe psychiatric disorders is still lacking.Importantly, agentic AI should be conceptualized as an intelligent co-pilot, designed to augment rather than override clinician expertise. By integrating evidence-based insights, comprehensive patient histories, and real-time data streams, agentic systems can assist psychiatrists in forming differential diagnoses and refining treatment strategies35. The ultimate decision-making authority, however, must remain firmly in the hands of human clinicians to preserve the nuance and contextual judgment central to psychiatric care. Failure to maintain human-in-the-loop safeguards risks automation bias, in which clinicians defer too readily to AI-generated suggestions.The potential impact of agentic AI is particularly compelling in low-resource and underserved settings, where shortages of mental health professionals are acute36. By autonomously managing tasks such as initial screenings, triaging, and delivering low-intensity digital interventions, agentic AI can expand access to care and alleviate pressure on overburdened systems. This aligns well with stepped-care models, where AI-driven agents provide early-stage support and escalate cases to human clinicians as needed37. However, stepped-care deployment also raises equity concerns: systems trained predominantly on high-income population data may perform poorly in marginalized groups, exacerbating disparities unless representative datasets and fairness audits are prioritized. Meaningful implementation in such settings further requires realistic technical and financial planning. Many functions can be supported by smaller distilled models or lighter rules-based components, with high-capacity models reserved for occasional complex reasoning, and hybrid architectures leveraging on-device processing to reduce connectivity demands. Ultimately, scalability in underserved contexts depends on dedicated infrastructure investment and policy support, making this an aspirational but attainable direction.Beyond these system-level considerations, agentic AI also presents opportunities for more clinically focused applications. One potential application of Agentic AI in psychiatry is real-time relapse prevention and crisis detection. Many psychiatric conditions, such as bipolar disorder, major depressive disorder, and schizophrenia, are characterized by cyclical patterns and relapse risk. By providing timely alerts to both patients and clinicians, these systems could enable rapid interventions, potentially preventing hospitalizations or severe exacerbations. Yet existing relapse detection tools demonstrate high rates of false positives and alert fatigue, suggesting that premature deployment of agentic systems in this domain could create new risks.Another frontier is personalized behavioral coaching and psychoeducation. Beyond symptom management, agentic AI could support everyday mental health maintenance by delivering individualized behavioral nudges, stress management exercises, and cognitive reframing strategies tailored to each person’s unique context. Such functions overlap with wellness technologies already in use, but evidence of durable impact in clinical populations remains weak.The evolution of agentic AI into mental health partners also carries significant societal implications. Widespread adoption could democratize access to high-quality mental health support, particularly in settings where professional resources are scarce. By extending the reach of mental health services into daily life, agentic AI has the potential to shift psychiatry from a reactive, episodic intervention model to a proactive, continuous care paradigm. Yet this shift poses critical questions: How will continuous monitoring affect patient autonomy? Who controls the data, and who is accountable for misuse? These concerns underscore the importance of regulatory oversight, participatory design, and robust ethical debate before any such vision is realized.System design and implementation roadmapSuccessfully integrating agentic AI into psychiatric practice requires a coordinated roadmap that brings together technical innovation, clinical validation, and policy development. The roadmap in Fig.3aoutlines the key milestones required for safe integration of agentic AI, spanning technical, clinical, and policy domains. To complement this, Fig.3bpresents a systems-level architecture that incorporates multi-agent orchestration, feedback loops, memory, personalization, and governance structures, critical to realizing the safe, scalable deployment of agentic AI in psychiatric care.Fig. 3: Implementation and systems roadmap for agentic AI in psychiatry.Full size imageaTechnical, clinical, and policy milestones for responsible deployment of agentic AI, including multimodal data integration, explainability frameworks, co-design pilots, safety trials, and adaptive regulatory models.bSystems-level blueprint for safe and trustworthy agentic AI. The three central horizontal blocks represent the progressive capability layers required for safe deployment of agentic AI in psychiatry: (i) foundational technical capacities for multi-agent reasoning and clinical workflow integration, (ii) governance and human-oversight mechanisms that modulate autonomy and ensure safety, and (iii) phased clinical evaluation and adoption strategies that validate real-world effectiveness and trust.cStaged clinical roadmap for the safe integration of agentic AI in psychiatry. The roadmap emphasizes phased progression, from narrow, low-risk pilots to large-scale pragmatic trials and long-term drift management, ensuring that safety, equity, and trust are systematically addressed alongside technical performance. LLM Large Language Model, MCP Model Context Protocol, FHIR Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources, HL7 Health Level 7, RAG Retrieval-Augmented Generation, RLHF Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback, RCT Randomized Controlled Trial, EHR Electronic Health Record, SHAP SHapley Additive exPlanations.Importantly, system architecture choices in agentic AI are not cognitively neutral. Decisions regarding when human review occurs, what level of abstraction is presented, and how recommendations are framed can substantially influence clinician judgment through automation bias, anchoring, and authority gradients. As such, automation bias must be treated as a first-class design constraint, rather than a downstream deployment risk. Architectural components should therefore be evaluated not only for performance and scalability, but also for their effects on human decision-making under time pressure and cognitive load.On the technical front, future agentic AI systems must be capable of integrating multimodal data streams, including text, audio, visual cues, and physiological signals, to build comprehensive, dynamic patient profiles. A reference architecture may include a multimodal data ingestion layer, a feature extraction and reasoning layer that combines large language model (LLM)–driven analysis with rule-based logic, and clinician-facing output interfaces integrated into the EHR to enable efficient review and intervention, with explicit control over review timing (pre-decision vs post-decision), representational granularity (raw data vs model abstractions), and confidence signaling to mitigate anchoring and over-reliance. Developing robust architectures that can continuously learn and adapt to evolving patient states, while avoiding issues like catastrophic forgetting, will be critical to ensuring personalized and contextually relevant support. Equally important is building explainable AI frameworks that facilitate shared decision-making. Balancing these competing demands is a central technical challenge.Clinically, the roadmap should be staged to ensure safe and incremental adoption (Fig.3c). In Phase I, narrow pilots should focus on low-risk applications such as documentation assistance and basic symptom monitoring, providing initial insights into usability and clinician acceptance. Phase II should then advance to safety and feasibility trials, evaluating not only accuracy but also patient engagement, clinician workload, and the preservation of therapeutic alliance. Phase II trials should therefore include adherence and acceptability as core evaluation endpoints. Adherence can be measured through uptake, sustained use, completion of recommended modules, response rates to prompts, time-on-task, dropout patterns, and re-engagement after lapses. These studies should also evaluate therapeutic alliance and perceived acceptability of human–AI collaboration using validated patient-reported measures. Phase III would involve pragmatic multi-site trials conducted across diverse populations, testing the generalizability of agentic AI systems and assessing their equity impacts. Finally, Phase IV should emphasize long-term monitoring and drift management, ensuring that models remain valid and trustworthy as populations, diagnostic criteria, and data sources evolve over time. These phases align conceptually with the four phases of clinical trials: Phase I focuses on small-scale safety and feasibility testing (typically tens of participants), Phase II evaluates early efficacy and safety in the target population with moderate samples, Phase III involves larger multisite pragmatic evaluations, and Phase IV centers on post-deployment surveillance and drift monitoring. In this framework, the agent’s capabilities are intentionally restricted in Phase I for example, operating offline or under tight clinician supervision with progressive expansion of autonomy and integration across Phases II–IV as evidence accumulates. The roadmap is intended both as a pipeline for a single agentic system to progress from prototype to clinical use and as a macro-level framework for evaluating, governing, and monitoring families of psychiatric agentic systems in the field.From a policy perspective, adaptive regulatory frameworks must evolve alongside technical and clinical advancements. Additionally, clear guidelines defining accountability and liability are needed to determine the respective responsibilities of AI developers, healthcare institutions, and clinicians in cases of adverse outcomes. Privacy-preserving techniques such as federated learning and differential privacy are promising but come with real trade-offs: federated learning may increase communication costs and degrade performance, while differential privacy can reduce model accuracy in detecting subtle psychiatric signals. Robust data governance standards are equally vital, ensuring that sensitive, continuously collected patient data is managed securely and ethically, with a strong emphasis on privacy and patient consent.Human-centered design and co-developmentFor agentic AI to be responsibly and effectively integrated into psychiatry, human-centered design principles must be prioritized from the outset. Co-design workshops with clinicians and patients can be conducted early to identify priorities and pain points. Iterative prototyping cycles, combined with usability testing in real-world clinical environments, allow rapid refinement of system interfaces. Lived-experience advisory panels can be convened to provide ongoing guidance on language, tone, and interaction design, ensuring that outputs resonate with diverse patient groups.In parallel, UI/UX design must be central to development. Interface elements such as dashboard layouts, color schemes, alert systems, and natural language feedback need to be tested for clarity, accessibility, and cultural appropriateness. For clinicians, interfaces should prioritize signal over noise, offering concise and interpretable outputs rather than overwhelming visualizations. For patients, design must emphasize simplicity, emotional safety, and inclusivity (such as, minimizing clinical jargon, offering multilingual or multimodal options).Central to this approach is trust-building. Iterative feedback mechanisms, such as built-in prompts for clinician input or patient corrections, can reinforce a sense of shared agency and foster bidirectional learning between human users and the AI system. We propose a human-AI interaction loop to capture these dynamics. By embedding co-design, participatory governance, and UI/UX integration throughout development and deployment, agentic AI can evolve as a true “co-pilot”, not in the sense of delegated authority, but as a deliberately constrained collaborator whose influence on human judgment is continuously monitored, audited, and adjusted.Clinical scenarios: assistive, collaborative, and semi-autonomous modelsMany of the capabilities described in agentic AI can also be approximated using non-agentic approaches, however, the added value of agentic systems lies in their ability to engage in multi-step planning and replanning, adapt to evolving symptoms and adherence patterns, proactively re-engage patients following disengagement, and coordinate information and actions across patients, carers, clinicians, and services (Table1). These capabilities become particularly important in longitudinal relapse prevention, stepped-care orchestration, or multi-stakeholder cases where one-off alerts or static rules are insufficient. At the same time, for simpler tasks such as basic screening or threshold-based monitoring, non-agentic approaches may remain preferable due to lower cost and complexity. Against this backdrop, the following clinical scenarios illustrate where agentic behavior can provide meaningful incremental value: assistive, collaborative, and semi-autonomous models of care.Table 1 Added value of agentic AI beyond non-agentic digital tools across key psychiatric applicationsFull size tableIn the assistive model (Fig.4A), agentic AI supports clinicians through personalized monitoring and decision support. For example, a patient with bipolar disorder may be monitored using speech recordings, wearable data, and self-reported mood inputs. The system detects early warning signs of a manic episode, such as linguistic changes and decreased sleep, and flags this to the clinician along with summarized insights. This allows for timely intervention while maintaining clinical oversight. Yet such monitoring systems risk generating false positives that could lead to unnecessary interventions or patient anxiety.Fig. 4: Clinical applications of agentic AI across three psychiatric care models.Full size imageAAssistive agents support clinicians for personalized management and disease monitoring.BCollaborative agents work alongside multidisciplinary teams by identifying symptoms, generating diagnostic suggestions, and proposing treatment plans validated by psychiatrists and coordinated with other professionals.CSemi-autonomous agents engage directly with patients for intake, triage, and structured interventions, with oversight mechanisms validation to ensure safety and contextual relevance. Created in BioRender. Bhat, V. (2025)https://BioRender.com/aul3qhw. REC Recording, eHR Electronic Health Record, DB Database, PHW Public Health Worker, CBT Cognitive Behavioral Therapy, SOAP Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan.In the collaborative model (Fig.4B), agentic AI contributes as a member of the care team. For a patient with both post-traumatic stress disorder and substance use disorder, the system might integrate clinical history, wearable data, and patient narratives to generate differential diagnoses and propose initial treatment options. These suggestions are reviewed during multidisciplinary rounds, with the AI updating its outputs based on clinician input. This supports shared decision-making across the care team while preserving clinician authority. However, there is a risk of automation bias if clinicians defer too readily to AI-generated outputs.In the semi-autonomous model (Fig.4C), agentic AI engages more directly with patients, particularly in settings with limited clinical resources. In a stepped-care framework, the system can conduct digital intake interviews, evaluate symptom severity using multimodal inputs, and triage patients to the appropriate level of care. For example, a patient might be directed to a digital cognitive behavioral therapy program or flagged for urgent psychiatric assessment. All decisions remain subject to clinician validation to ensure safety and contextual relevance. Importantly, these systems are designed as constrained, human-in-the-loop agents: all triage recommendations and risk-relevant decisions remain subject to clinician validation, and the system escalates automatically when encountering high-risk symptoms or uncertainty. If oversight mechanisms are weak, high-severity cases could be under-triaged, delaying critical care. In addition, adherence and acceptability must be empirically evaluated, as some patients, particularly those with severe psychiatric disorders may have difficulty engaging consistently with digital systems or may prefer direct clinician contact. Ethical concerns also arise around consent and transparency, wherein, patients must understand when they are interacting with AI rather than a clinician.Evaluation dimensionsEvaluating agentic AI in psychiatry should go well beyond conventional predictive performance (such as, accuracy or AUROC) because psychiatric care depends heavily on therapeutic relationships, variable adherence, and safety-sensitive decisions. A psychiatry-tailored evaluation framework should therefore report patient-level clinical effectiveness (symptom and functioning change on validated measures, relapse-related outcomes, and quality of life where appropriate) alongside safety and clinical appropriateness (adverse events and near-misses, false positives/negatives in monitoring and triage, and whether the agent’s recommendations and actions align with clinician judgment). For any system that interacts with patients, it is also essential to measure escalation behavior and safety overrides, that is how reliably the system routes high-risk situations to human care and what happens after escalation rather than assuming safe behavior from design alone.Because acceptability and adherence are often the limiting factors in real-world psychiatric deployment, evaluations should treat patient usability, acceptability, and adherence as core endpoints rather than secondary observations. This includes quantifying uptake, sustained use, completion of recommended modules/tasks, response rates to check-ins, dropout patterns, and re-engagement after lapses, and pairing these with patient-reported experience measures (perceived helpfulness, burden, trust, and perceived impact on autonomy). In parallel, studies should explicitly assess effects on therapeutic alliance and trust both the patient’s relationship with clinicians and the acceptability of human–AI collaboration since even a technically accurate system may be clinically counterproductive if it erodes alliance or increases disengagement.Finally, psychiatry-relevant evaluation must include clinician and system-level outcomes: clinician usability and workflow integration (documentation time, cognitive load, alert fatigue, editability/override rates, and perceived decision-support value), and service-level impact (access, wait times, continuity, initiation of treatment, and dropout). Equity and generalizability should be reported across demographic and clinical subgroups (including severity), alongside robustness and longitudinal validity (calibration, stability under distribution shift, drift monitoring and governance in post-deployment use). Where feasible, economic and sustainability considerations (hardware/software costs and operational costs of monitoring and governance) should be included so that “effectiveness” reflects real constraints on implementability, not only technical feasibility.Limitations, risks, and mitigation strategiesWhile agentic AI presents transformative potential in psychiatry, its adoption must be accompanied by a clear-eyed assessment of associated risks and limitations. As detailed in Table2, we outline a structured set of mitigation strategies to address each of these risks.Table 2Risk-mitigation summary table:A structured table summarizing key risks associated with agentic AI in psychiatry, their potential impacts, and proposed mitigation strategies including governance frameworks, bias audits, and human-centered oversight to ensure ethical and equitable deploymentFull size tableCrucially, past experiences show that many digital psychiatry tools have failed when deployed at scale. High attrition rates in chatbot trials, the premature withdrawal of NHS triage chatbots due to safety concerns, and the collapse of IBM Watson Oncology illustrate that technical promise does not guarantee clinical impact. These failures emphasize the importance of feasibility testing, user trust, and sociotechnical alignment. Key approaches include the implementation of routine bias audits38,39to evaluate model performance across demographic subgroups and ensure equity in decision support. Prioritizing diverse, representative training data is essential to reduce the perpetuation of existing systemic disparities. Transparent governance mechanisms, including oversight bodies with clearly defined accountability structures, are necessary to establish responsibility when AI systems influence diagnostic or therapeutic pathways40,41,42.Automation bias is another risk. Clinicians may over-rely on AI-generated outputs, particularly when presented in authoritative interfaces, leading to deskilling of clinical reasoning. Mitigation requires not only explainable AI interfaces but also formal training programs that teach clinicians to question and contextualize AI suggestions. Continuous monitoring of speech, activity, or wearable data may also erode patient autonomy and create feelings of being watched. Even if technically feasible, such systems risk undermining therapeutic alliance unless consent is ongoing, reversible, and supported by transparent data-use policies.Data privacy must be safeguarded through strict governance standards that emphasize informed consent, secure data storage, and limitations on secondary data use43. In parallel, clinician training programs should be developed to promote meaningful human oversight, ensuring that agentic AI augments rather than displaces clinical expertise44. Finally, economic constraints pose a practical limitation for deploying agentic AI, particularly in resource-constrained settings. LLM-based inference requires nontrivial hardware, compute, and storage infrastructure, while proprietary model licensing or cloud-based inference can incur sustained software and operational costs. Long-term expenses include data curation, audit logging, model monitoring, retraining, governance committees, and user support, all of which must be budgeted as part of implementation. As such, responsible deployment will require health-economic evaluation rather than assuming that technical feasibility alone guarantees sustainability.ConclusionThe integration of agentic AI into psychiatry represents both a transformative opportunity and a profound responsibility. To move forward safely and effectively, the field must focus on three core priorities: establishing standardized definitions and conceptual clarity, rigorously validating safety and efficacy through robust clinical trials, and developing comprehensive ethical and governance frameworks. These steps will help ensure that agentic AI systems enhance, rather than compromise, the quality and integrity of psychiatric care. Ultimately, the central question remains: should agentic AI serve as an assistive co-pilot under human supervision, or could it 1 day evolve toward more autonomous decision-making roles? By engaging thoughtfully and proactively, we can shape a future where agentic AI empowers both clinicians and patients, supporting a more personalized, preventive, and human-centered approach to mental health care.Data availabilityNo datasets were generated or analyzed during the current study.ReferencesElhaddad, M. & Hamam, S. AI-driven clinical decision support systems: an ongoing pursuit of potential.Cureus16, e57728 (2024).PubMedPubMed CentralGoogle ScholarDeivayanai, V. C. et al. Transforming healthcare: the impact of artificial intelligence on diagnostics, pharmaceuticals, and ethical considerations - a comprehensive review.Int. J. Surg. Lond. Engl.111, 4666–4693 (2025).ArticleCASGoogle ScholarDaemi, A. et al. Revolutionizing personalized medicine using artificial intelligence: a meta-analysis of predictive diagnostics and their impacts on drug development.Clin. Exp. Med.25, 255 (2025).ArticlePubMedPubMed CentralGoogle ScholarHagos, D. H. & Rawat, D. B. Recent advances in artificial intelligence and tactical autonomy: current status, challenges, and perspectives.Sensors22, 9916 (2022).ArticlePubMedPubMed CentralGoogle ScholarJoshi, S. A literature review of gen AI agents in financial applications: models and implementations.SSRN, [preprint],https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5133985(2025).Kaur, R., Gabrijelčič, D. & Klobučar, T. Artificial intelligence for cybersecurity: literature review and future research directions.Inf. Fusion97, 101804 (2023).Al Kuwaiti, A. et al. A review of the role of artificial intelligence in healthcare.J. Pers. Med.13, 951 (2023).ArticlePubMedPubMed CentralGoogle ScholarGao, S. et al. Empowering biomedical discovery with AI agents.Cell187, 6125–6151 (202